<BaseLayout title="Accessories" description="A/C, gas pedals, clutch master, and accessory drive notes for LS Gen Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Accessories</h1>
  <p>
    Accessory drive spacing, A/C retention, pedal choice, and clutch hydraulics all affect how "daily-able" the finished car feels.
  </p>

  <h2>A/C System</h2>
  <p>
    Wire colors for A/C request vary by year. Verify with power on and the A/C button pressed (12V and/or continuity at the cabin plug).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/ac-cabin-plug.jpg" alt="A/C control module plug in cabin with AC pins marked" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Plug to the A/C controls in the cabin — marked pins are the A/C request/control wires to verify with power on.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/ac-wires-on.jpg" alt="Yellow and blue A/C wires at passenger side connectors" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Yellow and blue wires at the passenger-side connectors — these are the A/C-on signals on many years (confirm on your car).
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>2010 2.0T example:</strong> Yellow (Pin 21 A/C select high) and Blue (Pin 22 A/C output high) on the A/C control module; same colors on the smaller passenger fuse-box plug.</li>
    <li><strong>2013 3.8:</strong> Same colors (yellow and blue) on the larger plug leading to the passenger-side fuse box.</li>
  </ul>

  <img src="/images/ac-2013-38-larger-plug.jpg" alt="2013 3.8 larger plug to passenger fuse box with yellow and blue A/C wires marked" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    2013 3.8 — larger plug to the passenger-side fuse box. Yellow and blue are the A/C wires (same colors as the 2.0T example).
  </p>
  <p>
    To keep stock A/C function: retain the fuse-box harness and ECU (you can still trim unused engine-control wires). Many hide the fuse box under a relocated battery.
    See also: <a href="https://www.gencoupe.com/threads/diy-battery-side-relay-box-relocation.60540/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DIY battery / relay box relocation</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Holley Terminator X path often uses the Current Performance Holley A/C add-on module harness, a Vintage Air 11079-VUS binary switch, and the matching pigtail.
    Low-mount F-body Holley A/C bracket setups are common; stock A/C hoses usually need to be shortened/re-ended at a hose shop (roughly $350 in one documented build).
  </p>

  <h3>Compressor mount &amp; hose routing</h3>
  <img src="/images/ac-engine-bay-overview.jpg" alt="LS swapped red Genesis Coupe engine bay showing A/C compressor and hose routing" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Overall bay view — low-mount A/C compressor on the passenger side with custom hose routing clear of the accessory drive and radiator.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/ac-compressor-closeup.jpg" alt="Close-up of A/C compressor fittings and hard lines" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Compressor fittings and hard-line adapters. Stock Genesis hoses are typically cut and re-crimped to fit the LS bracket layout.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/ac-compressor-clutch-hoses.jpg" alt="A/C compressor clutch and PolarSeal hose routing" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Clutch face and hose path along the frame rail — keep lines secured and away from belts, headers, and sharp edges.
  </p>

  <h2>Gas Pedals</h2>
  <h3>Cable throttle</h3>
  <p>
    Cable pedal installs are straightforward. Some builders start with a Nissan 240SX-style pedal, then fab a custom box for better placement with race shoes.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/custom-pedal-box.jpg" alt="Custom three-pedal setup with yellow painted firewall and cable throttle" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Community example — custom pedal box after the stock 240-style placement felt wrong with race shoes (Dominic Corelli).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/cable-pedal-firewall.jpg" alt="Cable throttle pedal mount at firewall with return spring" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Cable pedal mount at the firewall with return spring — keep the cable path smooth and free of sharp bends.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/cable-throttle-bracket.jpg" alt="Throttle cable bracket on transmission tunnel / firewall side" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Cable bracket on the tunnel / firewall side with adjuster — common way to land the cable cleanly at the throttle body.
  </p>

  <h3>Drive-by-wire (DBW)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Jason Riddle builds a popular pedal assembly for these cars.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>GM Genuine Parts 10379038</strong> accelerator pedal with position sensor is a common donor for Holley Terminator setups —
      <a href="https://a.co/d/8oOQHmO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon listing</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keeping the OEM Genesis pedal on Holley Terminator is possible using the pin map below (compiled from Liam Heuvel’s notes).
      <strong>Not 100% confirmed for every year/setup</strong> — verify with a multimeter and Holley software before finalizing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h4>OEM Genesis pedal → Holley (community map)</h4>
  <img src="/images/bk1-pedal-pinout.jpg" alt="BK1 accelerator pedal position sensor pinout" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">BK1 pedal pinout (factory).</p>

  <img src="/images/bk2-pedal-pinout.jpg" alt="BK2 accelerator pedal position sensor pinout" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">BK2 pedal pinout (factory) — wire colors differ from BK1.</p>

  <img src="/images/oem-pedal-holley-handwritten.jpg" alt="Handwritten Genesis to Holley pedal pin mapping BK1 and BK2" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Handwritten cross-reference: BK1/BK2 pin numbers and colors to Holley pedal connector A–F.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/holley-dbw-harness-diagram.jpg" alt="Holley DBW harness diagram for pedal and throttle body" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Holley DBW harness overview — pedal and throttle-body connectors (reference for which Holley pins to hit).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/gm-10379038-pedal.jpg" alt="GM Genuine Parts 10379038 accelerator pedal with position sensor" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    GM 10379038 DBW pedal — widely used with Holley Terminator X when not using a custom assembly.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/jason-riddle-dbw-closeup.jpg" alt="Jason Riddle DBW pedal assembly close-up with sensor connector" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Jason Riddle DBW pedal assembly — sensor body and mounting next to the clutch master area.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/jason-riddle-dbw-installed.jpg" alt="Jason Riddle DBW pedal installed in Genesis Coupe footwell" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
