  </p>

  <img src="/images/dustin-shoulta-dbw-pedal.jpg" alt="Dustin Shoulta DBW gas pedal install with floor brace" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Dustin Shoulta’s DBW gas pedal — braced to the floor for a solid mount next to the brake pedal.
  </p>

  <h2>Clutch Master Cylinder</h2>
  <p>
    Wilwood master cylinder with an ISR adapter is a common combination.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Add a thin rubber washer behind the clutch pedal pin and file the cylinder mounting face so it sits flat.</li>
    <li>Trim the Wilwood dust boot so it fits behind the adapter — this helps cylinder life.</li>
  </ul>

  <img src="/images/clutch-master-cylinder-pin.jpg" alt="Clutch pedal pin and master cylinder pushrod connection with plastic bushing" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Pedal pin and pushrod connection — thin washer / bushing behind the pin helps alignment and reduces wear.
  </p>

  <h2>ABS Delete / Brake Plumbing</h2>
  <p>
    Many LS swaps delete the factory ABS/ESC hydraulic unit and hardline straight from the master cylinder, with a proportioning valve and residual-pressure valves as needed.
    New front caliper lines are common; rear lines can often stay factory if the junctions are re-tied cleanly.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-mc-overview.jpg" alt="Master cylinder with proportioning valve and residual valves installed" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Master cylinder area after ABS delete — proportioning valve, residual valves, and hardlines.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-proportioning.jpg" alt="Close-up of proportioning valve and residual pressure valve stack" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Proportioning valve and residual-pressure valve plumbing at the master.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-mc-side.jpg" alt="Side view of master cylinder and valve block on firewall" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Side view of the valve block and lines on the firewall.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-wilwood-prop.jpg" alt="Wilwood proportioning valve with AN fittings" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Wilwood-style adjustable proportioning valve with AN fittings.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-assembly-bench.jpg" alt="Master cylinder and proportioning assembly on the bench" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Bench-built master + proportioning / residual assembly before install.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-assembly-hand.jpg" alt="Holding the proportioning valve assembly with residual valves" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Same assembly in hand — residual valves and hardline loops.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-hardline-install.jpg" alt="Hardlined master to front lines tied into factory rears" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Hardlined straight off the master, new front caliper lines, tied into factory rear lines (community example).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-delete-hardline-comment.jpg" alt="Community comment on hardlining off the master" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Community note: hardline off the master, new fronts, factory rears.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/abs-esc-hydraulic-diagram.jpg" alt="Factory ESC hydraulic system diagram" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
